## 1️⃣ Centralised error handling middleware  

### Why  
- Guarantees every error (sync, async, thrown or passed to `next`) is caught in one place.  
- Prevents stack traces or internal details from leaking to clients.  

### What to add  

```js
// errorHandler.js  (new file)
module.exports = function (err, req, res, next) {
  // Log the error (use a proper logger in production)
  console.error(err);

  // Choose an appropriate HTTP status code
  const status = err.statusCode || 500;

  // Do NOT expose err.message or stack in production
  const response = {
    error: status === 500 ? 'Internal Server Error' : err.message,
  };

  // Optionally add a request‑id for correlation
  if (req.id) response.requestId = req.id;

  res.status(status).json(response);
};
```

Then plug it into the app:

```js
const errorHandler = require('./errorHandler');

// ... all route definitions ...

// 404 handler (must be before errorHandler)
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const err = new Error('Not Found');
  err.statusCode = 404;
  next(err);
});

// Global error handler – last middleware
app.use(errorHandler);
```

### Async‑handler shortcut  

If you have many async route handlers, wrap them with a helper so you don’t need `try/catch` everywhere:

```js
// utils/asyncHandler.js
module.exports = fn => (req, res, next) => {
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
};
```

Usage:

```js
const asyncHandler = require('./utils/asyncHandler');

app.get('/users', asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
  const users = await User.find();      // example DB call
  res.json(users);
}));
```

