## 2️⃣ Patterns for **any future routes that accept user input**  

Even if your current code only serves read‑only data, you’ll likely add POST/PUT/PATCH endpoints later. Follow these practices from the start:

| Concern | Recommended library / pattern | Example |
|---------|------------------------------|---------|
| **Schema validation** (type, format, required fields) | **Joi**, **Yup**, **Zod** | ```js\nconst Joi = require('joi');\nconst userSchema = Joi.object({\n  name: Joi.string().min(2).max(100).required(),\n  email: Joi.string().email().required(),\n});\n\napp.post('/users', (req, res, next) => {\n  const { error, value } = userSchema.validate(req.body, { abortEarly: false });\n  if (error) return next(Object.assign(new Error('Invalid input'), { status: 400, details: error.details }));\n  // safe to use `value`\n  // … insert into DB …\n});\n``` |
| **Sanitize data** (prevent XSS, injection) | **express-validator** (sanitizers) or **DOMPurify** for HTML payloads | `body('email').trim().normalizeEmail()` |
| **Avoid async‑await errors leaking** | Wrap async route handlers in a helper that forwards rejections to Express’s error handler | ```js\nconst asyncWrap = fn => (req, res, next) => Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);\napp.post('/users', asyncWrap(async (req, res) => { /* … */ }));\n``` |
